    "result_text": "Green \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## How I addressed the findings\n\n**Main finding \u2014 duplicated `FY` label format + inaccurate javadoc (agreed, fixed).** The `FY` prefix and the two-digit segment formatting were living in two places. I centralised both in `OwnerFiscalYear`, which is the class that already claimed to own the fiscal-year convention:\n- Added `OwnerFiscalYear.yy(LocalDate)` \u2014 the one place the two-digit segment is produced.\n- Added `OwnerFiscalYear.label(String yy)` \u2014 the one place the `FY` prefix is applied; `label(LocalDate)` now delegates through `yy(...)` + `label(String)`.\n- `OwnerMemberId.memberId(...)` builds the `FY` segment via `OwnerFiscalYear.yy(...)`, and `OwnerMemberId.fiscalYearLabel(...)` now reconstructs the label via `OwnerFiscalYear.label(String)` instead of its own `\"FY\" + ...` literal.\n\nThe read-back genuinely resolves through `OwnerFiscalYear` again, so I kept (and sharpened) the `OwnerFiscalYear` javadoc \u2014 it now names the exact methods (`yy` for the memberId segment, `label(String)` for the read-back) and states plainly that the label format lives only there. The guarantee the comment asserts is now true.\n\n**Minor finding \u2014 dedup `-<n>` suffix vs. \"no separators\" (agreed, documented).** I left the inherited dedup behaviour in place (it's the established pattern and no behaviour change was warranted), but made the convention coherent: the OpenAPI description and `OwnerMemberId` class javadoc now say the four segments \"run together with no separators\" and explicitly call out that a `-<n>` collision suffix is the one exception, pointing at `deduplicate`.\n\nAll acceptance behaviour is unchanged (the member id still computes to e.g. `NSW2716E6609E1`).",
